activities.content:
In Module 1, remember how we wrote this?

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
}

// And then in Main:
Student s = new Student(); // Wait! Who wrote Student()?
```

We never wrote `Student()`, so why didn't the compiler complain? Because **the Java compiler silently created it for us!**

```java
// What you wrote:
class Student {
    String name;
}

// What the compiler actually compiles:
class Student {
    String name;

    // Generated silently by javac:
    Student() {
        super(); // Calls Object class
    }
}
```

### The Two Flavors of No-Arg Constructors:
1. **Default Constructor:** Provided automatically by the compiler. Invisible in source code. Takes zero parameters and does nothing except call `super()`.
2. **Explicit No-Arg Constructor:** Written by YOU in the code with zero parameters, where you specify default starting values.

### ⚠️ The #1 Beginner Trap: The Vanishing Constructor
Watch what happens when you add a constructor that requires a name:

```java
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String sName) {
        name = sName;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student("Aarav"); // Works!
        Student s2 = new Student(); // ❌ COMPILE ERROR: The constructor Student() is undefined!
    }
}
```

Why does `new Student()` fail now? Because the moment you defined `Student(String sName)`, Java retired its automatic default constructor! If you still want `new Student()`, you must explicitly write it yourself.

activities.codeSnippet:
class Book {
    String title;
    double price;

    // 1. Explicit No-Arg Constructor (provides safe fallback values)
    Book() {
        title = "Untitled Book";
        price = 0.0;
    }

    // 2. Custom Constructor (takes values at birth)
    Book(String bTitle, double bPrice) {
        title = bTitle;
        price = bPrice;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Both options are now fully supported!
        Book b1 = new Book(); // Uses explicit no-arg constructor
        Book b2 = new Book("Java Mastery", 29.99); // Uses custom constructor

        System.out.println("b1: " + b1.title + " - $" + b1.price);
        System.out.println("b2: " + b2.title + " - $" + b2.price);
    }
}

